    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d16">

                <pb facs="0064" n="30" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v08/pg30"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">764.00/11–2852: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 16<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Hungary (Ravndal) to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Budapest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-11-28T16:00:00+01:00">November 28, 1952—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>478. Dept’s attn drawn to lead article in Nov 21 <hi rendition="#italic">Cominform Journal</hi> on criticism and
                    self-criticism which would seem to be either a call for or preliminary
                    announcement of Slansky type purge in
                    other satellites. While emphasizing the value of criticism “at grass-root
                    level”, examples given point clearly to Pauker and Luca in Rumania
                    and Slansky group in Czecho. Exposure
                    right deviation in Rumania was “example of consistent application of principled
                    criticism”. Article goes on to say that criticism and self-criticism in People’s
                    Democracies “have not everywhere acquired necessary scale”.</p>
                <p>This would seem to point finger clearly at Hungary. Practically all accusations
                    against Slansky could be made against
                        Rakosi, Gero and/or other top leaders. Russian dissatisfaction with
                    economic progress and deliveries to Sov Union more and more open (re Legdes 271,
                    Oct 21<anchor n="1" xml:id="d16-f1"/>). Anti-Semitic angle particularly
                    pertinent here where top leadership almost solidly Jewish. Moreover, Gero, Farkas, Hazi, Gabor Peter, Nogradi and
                    probably others were in Spain.</p>
                <p>However, it can be argued that Rajk was Hung’s Slansky and local press repeatedly makes this point in comment
                    on Praha trial (Warsaw press apparently featuring Gomulka in same role). There is also problem of who would
                    replace top leaders who would be eliminated in purge. Many observers feel that
                    new group of younger men has not yet been trained. Rapid rise of Hisas is
                    perhaps significant in this respect.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Ravndal</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d16-f1" n="1" xml:id="d16-f1-content">
                <p>Despatch 271 provided a summary of political, military, and economic developments
                    in Hungary during the third quarter of 1952. (764.00/10–2152)</p>
            </note>
